00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:03,379
part 2 chapter 4.

2
00:00:03,379 --> 00:00:08,000
Winston looked around the shabby little room above Mr Charrington's shop.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Beside the window the enormous bed was made up, with ragged blankets and a coverless bolster.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,100
The old-fashioned clock with the 12-hour face was ticking away on the mantelpiece.

5
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:23,299
In the corner, on the gate leg table, the glass paperweight which he had bought on

6
00:00:23,299 --> 00:00:27,620
his last visit gleamed softly out of the half Darkness

7
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:32,299
In the fender was a batter to tin oil stove a saucepan and two cups, provided

8
00:00:32,299 --> 00:00:38,000
by Mr cherrington Winston lit the burner and set a pan of water to boil.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,500
He had brought an envelope full of Victory Coffee and some saccharine tablets.

10
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:47,299
The clock's hands said 1720 it was 1920 really.

11
00:00:47,299 --> 00:00:50,359
She was coming at 1930.

12
00:00:50,359 --> 00:00:56,359
Folly, folly, his heart kept saying: conscious, gratuitous, suicidal folly. Of

13
00:00:56,359 --> 00:01:01,579
all the crimes that a Party member could commit, this one was the least possible to conceal.

14
00:01:01,579 --> 00:01:06,020
Actually the idea had first floated into his head in the form of a vision of the

15
00:01:06,020 --> 00:01:09,318
glass paperweight mirrored by the surface of the gate leg table.

16
00:01:09,318 --> 00:01:14,420
As he had foreseen, Mr Charrington had made no difficulty about letting the room.

17
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:18,680
He was obviously glad of the few dollars that it would bring him. Nor did he seem

18
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:21,799
shocked or become offensively knowing when it was made clear that Winston

19
00:01:21,799 --> 00:01:24,920
wanted the room for the purpose of a love affair.

20
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:28,279
Instead he looked into the middle distance and spoke in generalities, with

21
00:01:28,279 --> 00:01:33,020
so delicate and heirs to give the impression that he had become partly invisible

22
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:35,959
the Privacy, he said, was a very valuable thing.

23
00:01:35,959 --> 00:01:39,500
Everyone wanted a place where they could be alone occasionally.

24
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:43,398
And when they had such a place, it was only common courtesy in anyone else who

25
00:01:43,398 --> 00:01:46,219
knew of it to keep his knowledge to himself.

26
00:01:46,219 --> 00:01:51,439
He even, seeming almost to fade out of existence as he did so, added that there

27
00:01:51,439 --> 00:01:57,459
were two entries to the house, one of them threw the backyard which gave on an alley.

28
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:00,079
Under the window somebody was singing.

29
00:02:00,079 --> 00:02:04,340
Winston peeped out, secure in the protection of the muslin curtain.

30
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:09,318
The June sun was still high in the sky, and in the sun-filled court below a

31
00:02:09,318 --> 00:02:13,879
monstrous woman, solid as a Norman pillar, with brawny red forearms and a sacking

32
00:02:13,879 --> 00:02:18,139
apron strapped about her middle, was stumping to and fro between a wash tub

33
00:02:18,139 --> 00:02:22,100
and a clothesline pegging out a series of square white things which Winston

34
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:25,040
recognized as babies' diapers.

35
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:30,680
Whenever her mouth was not corked with clothes pegs she was singing in a powerful contralto:

36
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:36,439
It was only an hopeless fancy, It passed like an April die But a look and a word

37
00:02:36,439 --> 00:02:43,279
in the dreams they stirred They have stolen my heart away The Dune had been haunting London for

38
00:02:43,279 --> 00:02:47,180
weeks past. It was one of countless similar songs published for the benefit

39
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:50,478
of the Pearls by a subsection of the Music Department.

40
00:02:50,478 --> 00:02:54,079
The words of these songs were composed without any human intervention whatever

41
00:02:54,079 --> 00:02:57,500
on an instrument known as a versificator.

42
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:02,540
But the woman sang so tunefully is to turn the dreadful rubbish into an almost pleasant sound.

43
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:06,139
He could hear the woman singing and the scrape of her shoes on the flagstones,

44
00:03:06,139 --> 00:03:09,799
and the cries of the children in the street, and somewhere in the far distance

45
00:03:09,799 --> 00:03:14,359
a faint roar of traffic, and yet the room seemed curiously silent, thanks to the

46
00:03:14,359 --> 00:03:17,238
absence of a telescreen.

47
00:03:17,238 --> 00:03:22,100
Folly, folly, folly! he thought again he It was inconceivable that they could

48
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:25,879
frequent this place for more than a few weeks without being caught.

49
00:03:25,879 --> 00:03:29,959
But the temptation of having a hiding place that was truly their own, indoors

50
00:03:29,959 --> 00:03:33,199
and near at hand, had been too much for both of them.

51
00:03:33,199 --> 00:03:36,738
For some time after their visit to the church belfry it had been impossible to

52
00:03:36,738 --> 00:03:42,079
arrange meetings. Working hours had been drastically increased in anticipation of Hate Week.

53
00:03:42,079 --> 00:03:46,279
It was more than a month distant, but the enormous, complex preparations that it

54
00:03:46,279 --> 00:03:49,639
entailed were throwing extra work onto everybody.

55
00:03:49,639 --> 00:03:53,840
Finally both of them managed to secure a free afternoon on the same day.

56
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:56,898
They had agreed to go back to the clearing in the wood.

57
00:03:56,898 --> 00:04:00,318
On the evening beforehand they met briefly in the street.

58
00:04:00,318 --> 00:04:04,398
As usual Winston hardly looked at Julia as they drifted towards one another in

59
00:04:04,398 --> 00:04:08,420
the crowd, but from the short glance he gave her it seemed to him that she was

60
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:10,040
paler than usual.

61
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:16,040
'It's all off,' she murmured as soon as she judged it safe to speak. 'Tomorrow, I mean.'

62
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:17,359
'What?'

63
00:04:17,359 --> 00:04:20,180
'Tomorrow afternoon. I can't come.'

64
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:21,859
'Why not?'

65
00:04:21,859 --> 00:04:26,478
'Oh, the usual reason it started early this time.'

66
00:04:26,478 --> 00:04:28,760
For a moment he was violently angry.

67
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:32,478
During the month that he had known her the nature of his desire for her had

68
00:04:32,478 --> 00:04:36,859
changed. At the beginning there had been little true sensuality in it.

69
00:04:36,859 --> 00:04:40,279
Their first love- making had been simply an act of the will.

70
00:04:40,279 --> 00:04:42,920
But after the second time it was different.

71
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:46,879
The smell of her hair, the taste of her mouth, the feeling of her skin seemed to

72
00:04:46,879 --> 00:04:50,359
have got inside him, or into the air all around him.

73
00:04:50,359 --> 00:04:54,920
She had become a physical necessity, something that he not only wanted but

74
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:59,238
felt that he had a right to. When she said that she could not come, he had the

75
00:04:59,238 --> 00:05:01,639
feeling that she was cheating him.

76
00:05:01,639 --> 00:05:06,318
But just at this moment the crowd pressed them together and their hands accidentally met.

77
00:05:06,318 --> 00:05:10,040
She gave the tips of his fingers a quick squeeze that seemed to invite not to

78
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:14,898
desire but affection struck him that when one lived with a woman this particular disappointment

79
00:05:14,898 --> 00:05:20,180
must be a normal, recurring event; and a deep tenderness, such as he had not felt

80
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:23,420
for her before, suddenly took hold of him.

81
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:27,318
He wished that they were a married couple of ten years' standing. He wished

82
00:05:27,318 --> 00:05:30,559
that he were walking through the streets with her just as they were doing now, but

83
00:05:30,559 --> 00:05:34,760
openly and without fear, talking of trivialities and buying odds and ends

84
00:05:34,760 --> 00:05:38,420
for the household. He wished above all that they had some place where they

85
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:43,219
could be alone together without feeling the obligation to make love every time they met.

86
00:05:43,219 --> 00:05:46,939
It was not actually at that moment, but at some time on the following day, that

87
00:05:46,939 --> 00:05:50,719
the idea of renting Mr cherrington's room had occurred to him.

88
00:05:50,719 --> 00:05:55,159
When he suggested it to Julia she had agreed with unexpected readiness.

89
00:05:55,159 --> 00:05:57,680
Both of them knew that it was lunacy.

90
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:01,818
It was as though they were intentionally stepping nearer to their graves.

91
00:06:01,818 --> 00:06:05,359
As he sat waiting on the edge of the bed he thought again of the sellers of the

92
00:06:05,359 --> 00:06:09,738
Ministry of Love. It was curious how that predestined horror moved in and out of

93
00:06:09,738 --> 00:06:11,120
one's consciousness.

94
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:16,100
There it lay, fixed in future times preceding death as surely as 99 precedes

95
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:21,020
100.. One could not avoid it, but one could perhaps postpone it: and yet

96
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:24,920
instead, every now and again, by a conscious willful act, one chose to

97
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:28,359
shorten the interval before it happened.

98
00:06:28,398 --> 00:06:33,020
At this moment there was a quick step on the stairs. Julia burst into the room. She

99
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:37,340
was carrying a tool bag of coarse brown canvas, such as he had sometimes seen her

100
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:41,540
carrying to and fro at the Ministry. He started forward to take her in his arms,

101
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:47,000
but she disengaged herself rather hurriedly, partly because she was still holding the tool bag

102
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,959
Half a second,' she said. 'Just let me show you what I've brought.

103
00:06:50,959 --> 00:06:54,020
Did you bring some of that filthy Victory Coffee?

104
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:57,439
I thought you would. You can chuck it away again, because we shouldn't be

105
00:06:57,439 --> 00:06:59,540
needing it. Look here.'

106
00:06:59,540 --> 00:07:03,559
She fell on her knees, threw open the bag and tumbled out some spanners and a

107
00:07:03,559 --> 00:07:06,079
screwdriver that filled the top part of it.

108
00:07:06,079 --> 00:07:08,840
Underneath were a number of neat paper packets.

109
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:13,459
The first packet that she passed to Winston had a strange and yet vaguely familiar feeling.

110
00:07:13,459 --> 00:07:19,459
It was filled with some kind of heavy sand like stuff which yielded wherever you touched it.

111
00:07:19,459 --> 00:07:21,799
'It isn't sugar? he said.

112
00:07:21,799 --> 00:07:24,859
'Real sugar. Not saccharin sugar.

113
00:07:24,859 --> 00:07:29,359
And here's a loaf of bread— —proper white bread, not our bloody stuff— —and a little

114
00:07:29,359 --> 00:07:32,238
pot of jam. And here's a tin of milk— —but

115
00:07:32,238 --> 00:07:33,978
look! This is the one I'm really proud of.

116
00:07:33,978 --> 00:07:36,559
I had to wrap a bit of sacking around it, because———'

117
00:07:36,559 --> 00:07:40,579
But she did not need to tell him why she had wrapped it up. The smell was already

118
00:07:40,579 --> 00:07:44,600
filling the room, a rich hot smell which seemed like an emanation from his early

119
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:48,978
childhood, but which one did occasionally meet with even now, blowing down a

120
00:07:48,978 --> 00:07:52,818
passageway before a door slammed, or diffusing itself mysteriously in a

121
