219
00:14:07,699 --> 00:14:13,520
'Dearest! You've gone quite pale. What's the matter? Do they make you feel sick?'

220
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:17,299
'Of all the horrors in the world— —a rat!'

221
00:14:17,299 --> 00:14:20,600
She pressed herself against him and wound her limbs around him, as though to

222
00:14:20,600 --> 00:14:23,059
reassure him with the warmth of her body.

223
00:14:23,059 --> 00:14:25,699
He did not reopen his eyes immediately.

224
00:14:25,699 --> 00:14:28,879
For several moments he had had the feeling of being back in a nightmare

225
00:14:28,879 --> 00:14:32,238
which had recurred from time to time throughout his life.

226
00:14:32,238 --> 00:14:36,559
It was always very much the same. He was standing in front of a wall of darkness,

227
00:14:36,559 --> 00:14:40,040
and on the other side of it there was something unendurable, something too

228
00:14:40,040 --> 00:14:44,180
dreadful to be faced. In the dream his deepest feeling was always one of

229
00:14:44,180 --> 00:14:49,459
self-deception, because he did in fact know what was behind the wall of darkness.

230
00:14:49,459 --> 00:14:53,180
With a deadly effort, like wrenching a piece out of his own brain, he could even

231
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:55,639
have dragged the thing into the open.

232
00:14:55,639 --> 00:15:00,620
He always woke up without discovering what it was somehow it was connected with what Julia

233
00:15:00,620 --> 00:15:03,020
had been saying when he cut her short.

234
00:15:03,020 --> 00:15:07,459
'I'm sorry,' he said; 'it's nothing. I don't like rats, that's all.'

235
00:15:07,459 --> 00:15:11,540
'Don't worry, dear, we're not going to have the filthy brutes in here. I'll stuff the

236
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:13,159
hole with a bit of sacking before we go.

237
00:15:13,159 --> 00:15:18,738
And next time we come here I'll bring some plaster and bang it up properly.'

238
00:15:18,738 --> 00:15:24,020
Already the black incident of panic was half forgotten Feeling slightly ashamed of himself, he

239
00:15:24,020 --> 00:15:28,159
sat up against the Bed Head Julia got out of bed, pulled on her

240
00:15:28,159 --> 00:15:30,379
overalls and made the coffee.

241
00:15:30,379 --> 00:15:34,219
The smell that rose from the saucepan was so powerful and exciting that they

242
00:15:34,219 --> 00:15:38,840
shut the window lest anybody outside should notice it and become inquisitive.

243
00:15:38,840 --> 00:15:42,559
What was even better than the taste of the coffee was the silky texture given

244
00:15:42,559 --> 00:15:48,139
to it by the sugar, a thing Winston had almost forgotten after years of saccharine.

245
00:15:48,139 --> 00:15:52,040
With one hand in her pocket and a piece of bread and jam in the other, Julia

246
00:15:52,040 --> 00:15:55,818
wandered about the room, glancing in differently at the bookcase pointing out

247
00:15:55,818 --> 00:15:59,540
the best way of repairing the gate leg table, plumping herself down in the

248
00:15:59,540 --> 00:16:03,439
ragged arm chair to see if it was comfortable, and examining the absurd

249
00:16:03,439 --> 00:16:06,500
12-hour clock with a sort of tolerant amusement.

250
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:10,398
She brought the glass paperweight over to the bed to have a look at it in a

251
00:16:10,398 --> 00:16:11,600
better light.

252
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:15,620
He took it out of her hand, fascinated, as always, by the soft rain watery

253
00:16:15,620 --> 00:16:17,719
appearance of the glass.

254
00:16:17,719 --> 00:16:20,840
'What is it, do you think?' said Julia.

255
00:16:20,840 --> 00:16:24,318
'I don't think it's anything— —I mean, I don't think it was ever put to any use.

256
00:16:24,318 --> 00:16:27,859
That's what I like about it. It's a little chunk of history that they've

257
00:16:27,859 --> 00:16:33,020
forgotten to alter. It's a message from a hundred years ago, if one knew how to read it.'

258
00:16:33,020 --> 00:16:37,100
'And that picture over there'— '—she nodded at the engraving on the opposite wall—'

259
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:40,040
—'would that be a hundred years old?'

260
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:48,260
'More 200 ideas say. One can't tell. It's impossible to discover the age of anything nowadays.'

261
00:16:48,260 --> 00:16:50,059
She went over to look at it.

262
00:16:50,059 --> 00:16:53,658
'Here's where that brute stuck his nose out,' she said, kicking away and squirting

263
00:16:53,658 --> 00:16:55,579
immediately below the picture.

264
00:16:55,579 --> 00:16:59,600
'What is this place? I've seen it before somewhere.'

265
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:05,059
'It's a church, or at least it used to be some Clement Danes its name was.'

266
00:17:05,059 --> 00:17:08,000
The fragment of rhyme that Mr cherrington had taught him came back

267
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:13,398
into his head, and he added half nostalgically Oranges and lemons," say the bells are

268
00:17:13,398 --> 00:17:17,478
Saint Clements To his astonishment she kept the line:

269
00:17:17,478 --> 00:17:21,439
'You owe me three farthings,' say the bells of Saint Martins When will you pay me?'

270
00:17:21,439 --> 00:17:25,398
say the bells of Old Bailey— 'I can't remember how it goes on after

271
00:17:25,398 --> 00:17:30,020
that. But anyway I remember it ends up, "Here comes a candle to light you to bed,

272
00:17:30,020 --> 00:17:34,040
here comes a chopper to chop off your head!"'

273
00:17:34,040 --> 00:17:38,359
It was like the two halves of a counter sign But there must be another line after 'the

274
00:17:38,359 --> 00:17:43,699
bells of Old bayley Perhaps it could be dug out of Mr Charrington's memory, if he was suitably

275
00:17:43,699 --> 00:17:45,439
prompted.

276
00:17:45,439 --> 00:17:47,898
'Who taught you that?' he said.

277
00:17:47,898 --> 00:17:52,219
'My grandfather. He used to say it to me when I was a little girl. He was

278
00:17:52,219 --> 00:17:55,639
vaporized when I was eight— —at any rate, he disappeared.

279
00:17:55,639 --> 00:18:00,859
I wonder what a lemon was,' she added inconsequently. 'I've seen oranges. They're

280
00:18:00,859 --> 00:18:04,520
a kind of round yellow fruit with a thick skin.'

281
00:18:04,520 --> 00:18:09,199
'I can remember lemons,' said Winston. 'They were quite common in the 50s They were

282
00:18:09,199 --> 00:18:13,000
so sour that it set your teeth on edge even to smell them.'

283
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:17,959
'I bet that picture's got bugs behind it,' said Julia. 'I'll take it down and give it

284
00:18:17,959 --> 00:18:22,520
a good clean someday I suppose it's almost time we were leaving. I must start

285
00:18:22,520 --> 00:18:27,620
washing this paint off. What a boar I'll get the lipstick off your face afterwards.'

286
00:18:27,620 --> 00:18:30,500
Winston did not get up for a few minutes more.

287
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:34,520
The room was darkening. He turned over towards the light and leg gazing into

288
00:18:34,520 --> 00:18:36,439
the glass paperweight.

289
00:18:36,439 --> 00:18:39,799
The inexhaustibly interesting thing was not the fragment of coral but the

290
00:18:39,799 --> 00:18:42,079
interior of the glass itself.

291
00:18:42,079 --> 00:18:46,040
There was such a depth of it, and yet it was almost as transparent as air.

292
00:18:46,040 --> 00:18:49,219
It was as though the surface of the glass had been the arch of the sky,

293
00:18:49,219 --> 00:18:52,818
enclosing a tiny world with its atmosphere complete.

294
00:18:52,818 --> 00:18:57,079
He had the feeling that he could get inside it, and that in fact he was inside

295
00:18:57,079 --> 00:19:01,579
it, along with the mahogany bed and the gate leg table, and the clock and the

296
00:19:01,579 --> 00:19:04,158
steel engraving and the paperweight itself.

297
00:19:04,158 --> 00:19:08,120
The paperweight was the room he was in, and the coral was Julia's life in his

298
00:19:08,120 --> 00:19:12,000
own, fixed in a sort of eternity at the heart of the crystal
